    "result_text": "The build is green \u2014 the full suite passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization on owner creation, structured across three small units following the existing `EmailNormalizer`/`TelephoneNormalizer` pattern.\n\n**New: `AddressNormalizer.java`** \u2014 a package-private helper that owns the algorithm and nothing else: trim, collapse internal whitespace, upper-case, and expand whole-word abbreviations (`ST`\u2192`STREET`, `RD`\u2192`ROAD`, `AVE`\u2192`AVENUE`). It's idempotent and returns `\"\"` for a null/blank input so callers treat blank-after-normalization uniformly.\n\n**`ValidateOwnerFields.java`** \u2014 normalizes the address in place *before* the required-field check. This is the single right place because:\n- The required-field check now rejects an address that is blank after normalization (the `\"   \"` \u2192 400 case).\n- The DTO is republished with the canonical address, so it flows unchanged through `BuildOwner` \u2192 the stored `Owner` \u2192 the `GET` response (the `\"12 MAIN STREET\"` / `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"` cases).\n\nIt delegates the actual transform to `AddressNormalizer`, so validation doesn't accumulate normalization logic.\n\n**`Households.java`** \u2014 the address portion of its comparison key now runs through `AddressNormalizer.normalize`, so both household duplicate detection (`EnsureUniqueHousehold`) and the shared household id (`AssignHousehold`, via `householdId`) compare the normalized form. This also makes matching robust against any pre-existing owners whose stored addresses aren't normalized. I renamed its private `normalize` to `normalizeName` since it now only handles the last name.\n\nNo changes to the pipeline wiring, DTOs, mapper, or exception handling were needed \u2014 a blank normalized address reuses the existing `MissingOwnerFieldsException` \u2192 400 path.\n\nTests: `AcceptanceTest` (3 tests) and the full suite pass \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
